  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 151 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe broader synoptic pattern will remain fairly stagnant over the \nnext several days, but weak progression of the longwave pattern, \nalong with downstream impacts of low passages over the \nNorthern/Canadian Plains will still create slight fluctuations to \nsurface conditions throughout the week. \n\nThe main impact over the next 48 hours is likely going to be fog \ndevelopment late tonight into tomorrow morning. Deep ridging aloft \nis becoming cutoff from the upper level flow, leading to a broad \narea of high pressure aloft. This has lead to modest subsidence and \nbuilding pressure near the surface of which is sinking southward \nahead of a weak frontal passage ahead of upstream lows. This will \neventually shift winds to the east, but in the meantime winds will \nstart to calm around dusk. Areas along and south of I-70 will have \nthe most prolonged period of calm winds leading to a greater \nlikelihood of fog development. Given scattered upper level clouds \nand very dry surface conditions, there is some uncertainty in the \ncoverage of fog, but at a minimum river valleys and well irrigated \nfields will likely see 1/2mi or less visibilities.\n\nThe other forecast impact for this week will be increasing winds \nWednesday through Friday. By Wednesday, the mid to upper trough will \nnear from the west leading to more unified SW flow, an increase in \nthe pressure gradient and greater diurnal mixing. All of these \ncombined will likely lead to gusty conditions each afternoon with \nsustained winds around 8-12kt with gusts around 20kt. This greater \nSW flow will also provide a slight warming trend with afternoon \nhighs approaching 80 later this week. \n\nAny lingering upper ridging will exit as multiple upper troughs move \nthrough next weekend. Uncertainty remains high on the details, but \nfor now it appears that the best forcing and moisture will arrive \novernight Saturday night into Sunday morning, with only low chances \nfor rain during Halloween evening events.\n\nIf the front remains north, highs will be in the 70s. However, a \nmore southern front as well as any rain would keep readings lower, \nespecially on Sunday.",
